  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "",
      "kind": "other",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Maria L. Okafor, David Chen, Priya Raman"
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Abstract",
      "kind": "abstract",
      "paragraphs": [
        "We examined whether extended sleep improves working-memory performance. In a randomized controlled trial (n = 96), participants who slept nine hours outperformed a control group (p < 0.001, 95% CI: 2.1 to 4.8). Prior work has been mixed (Kim, 2018; Alvarez & Novak, 2021)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "kind": "introduction",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Working memory is central to reasoning. Earlier studies suggested a link between sleep and cognition, but effect sizes varied widely. Okafor et al. (2019) reported a moderate effect, whereas Delgado (2017) found none."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Methods",
      "kind": "methods",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Ninety-six healthy adults were randomized into two groups (n = 96). We used an independent t-test to compare group means and a one-way ANOVA to assess dose response across three sleep durations. Categorical outcomes were analyzed with a chi-square test."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Results",
      "kind": "results",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Table 1 reports the primary outcomes. The extended-sleep group scored higher on the n-back task (p < 0.001, 95% CI: 2.1 to 4.8). A secondary analysis showed a dose effect (p = 0.02). These findings replicate Okafor et al. (2019)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Discussion",
      "kind": "discussion",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Our results support a causal role for sleep in working memory. The effect was robust across analyses, consistent with earlier reports (Alvarez & Novak, 2021)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Conclusion",
      "kind": "conclusion",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Extended sleep improves working memory in healthy adults."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "References",
      "kind": "references",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Kim, S. (2018). Sleep and attention across the lifespan. Journal of Cognitive Neuroscience, 30(4), 512-528. https://doi.org/10.1162/jocn.2018.00412",
        "Alvarez, R., & Novak, T. (2021). Rest and recall: a meta-analysis. Psychological Bulletin, 147(2), 100-130.",
        "Okafor, M., Chen, D., & Raman, P. (2019). Sleep extension and cognition. Sleep Research, 8(1), 22-39. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sleepr.2019.01.003"
      ]
    }
